The edges and vertices of a convex polyhedron, projected onto a plane from a point just outside one face, form a connected plane graph whose faces correspond to the faces of the polyhedron, so $V - E + F = 2$.

Every face has $p$ edges and every edge lies on two faces, so $pF = 2E$. Every vertex has $q$ edges and every edge has two ends, so $qV = 2E$. Substituting $F = \frac{2E}{p}$ and $V = \frac{2E}{q}$:
$$\frac{2E}{q} - E + \frac{2E}{p} = 2 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \frac1p + \frac1q = \frac12 + \frac1E > \frac12.$$
If $p, q \ge 4$ then $\frac1p + \frac1q \le \frac12$, impossible, so one of them is $3$. If $p = 3$ then $\frac1q > \frac16$, so $q \in \{3, 4, 5\}$, and symmetrically for $q = 3$. The solutions $(p, q)$ and the resulting $E$, then $V$ and $F$, are:

- $(3,3)$: $E = 6$, $V = 4$, $F = 4$, the tetrahedron;
- $(4,3)$: $E = 12$, $V = 8$, $F = 6$, the cube;
- $(3,4)$: $E = 12$, $V = 6$, $F = 8$, the octahedron;
- $(5,3)$: $E = 30$, $V = 20$, $F = 12$, the dodecahedron;
- $(3,5)$: $E = 30$, $V = 12$, $F = 20$, the icosahedron.

All five exist, so the list is complete.
